# libqrencode 4.1.1
#
# systemd-cryptenroll prints recovery keys as QR codes; bsod shows one too.
#
# Built with cmake rather than ./configure, which is not a preference. fukuchi.org
# stopped serving the autotools dist tarball and the GitHub release carries no
# asset, so manifest/sources.lock points at the git archive -- and a git archive
# has configure.ac but no generated `configure`. Running autoreconf is not an
# option either: it matches no fingerprint in pm's table, so the step would be
# refused before it ran (C2). The CMakeLists.txt in the same tarball builds the
# same library, and `cmake` is already a host requirement.
name: libqrencode
version:
- '4'
- '1'
- '1'
dependencies: []
steps:

- stage: Prepare
  name: fetch
  dl_urls:
    @URL_LIBQRENCODE@: '@SHA256_LIBQRENCODE@'
  run:
  - mkdir -p /build/src/libqrencode /build/b/libqrencode
  # --no-same-owner: pm's jail is a user namespace mapping only uid 0, so a
  # tarball recording any other uid fails tar at the very end.
  - tar -xf @DL_LIBQRENCODE@ --no-same-owner -C /build/src/libqrencode --strip-components=1

# Three options carry the whole configuration, and each is a default this build
# cannot take:
#
#   * BUILD_SHARED_LIBS defaults to NO here -- unusually -- so without it the
#     package installs libqrencode.a and nothing links against it.
#   * WITH_TOOLS defaults to YES and builds the qrencode command, which pulls in
#     libpng. libpng is not in this layer, so find_package(PNG) would reach past
#     the sysroot into pm's read-only mirror of the host's /usr and link the
#     build host's copy. Nothing here needs the command; systemd wants the
#     library.
#   * WITH_TESTS stays NO, which also keeps the internal symbols static.
#
# CMAKE_C_FLAGS is a clang @response-file, not a flag list: pm splits a command
# on whitespace, so a multi-word flag string would arrive as separate arguments.
# No CXX variables, because the project declares LANGUAGES C and cmake warns
# about manually-specified variables it never used.
# CMAKE_POLICY_DEFAULT_CMP0056=NEW is what makes the compiler work at all
# here, and the failure without it names the wrong thing entirely:
#
#     -- Check for working C compiler: /usr/bin/clang - broken
#     /usr/bin/ld: cannot find crtbeginS.o: No such file or directory
#     /usr/bin/ld: cannot find -lgcc: No such file or directory
#
# which reads as a broken toolchain rather than as a dropped flag. CMP0056 is
# "try_compile() honours CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS", introduced in CMake 3.2, and
# a project is given the OLD behaviour when its own cmake_minimum_required is
# older than that. libqrencode's CMakeLists.txt:1 asks for 3.1.0, so every
# try_compile -- including the compiler sanity check CMake runs before anything
# else -- silently drops the linker flags we passed and links with the
# defaults.
#
# That matters because --rtlib=compiler-rt, --unwindlib=libunwind and
# -fuse-ld=mold live in the LDFLAGS response file and nowhere else. Without
# them clang falls back to libgcc and the host ld, and neither the GCC runtime
# nor its crt objects exist in this sysroot -- hence crtbeginS.o and -lgcc
# rather than anything about qrencode. The probe is the only thing that fails;
# the real build would have linked correctly, which is what makes this so easy
# to misread.
#
#
# CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 is a third thing the same line 1 causes, and
# it appeared only when the build host became Arch. CMake 4 removed
# compatibility with pre-3.5 policy versions outright, so a `project()` whose
# cmake_minimum_required asks for less is now an error rather than a
# deprecation warning:
#
#     CMake Error at CMakeLists.txt:1 (cmake_minimum_required):
#       Compatibility with CMake < 3.5 has been removed from CMake.
#
# Debian trixie's CMake 3.31 still accepted it, which is why this tree carried
# the recipe for months without noticing. The variable is CMake's own suggested
# remedy and was added in 3.31, so it is understood by the last base as well as
# this one; an older CMake would report it as a manually-specified variable it
# did not use, not as an error.
#
# The same two recipes need it as need CMP0056, and for once that is not a
# coincidence: 3.5 is above 3.2, so anything already below the CMP0056 floor is
# below this one too. libcbor's recipe carries that sweep; compiler-rt and
# libunwind ask for 3.20 and were not in it.
#
# NEW rather than dropping the probe (CMAKE_TRY_COMPILE_TARGET_TYPE=
# STATIC_LIBRARY, as compiler-rt does) because the flags here are correct and
# should be tested. compiler-rt's case is a genuine bootstrap -- it is building
# the runtime a link would need -- and this is not.
#
# The other cmake packages in this tree do not need it: libfido2 asks for 3.7
# and the rest are at or above 3.2, so they already get NEW. Left per-recipe
# rather than made global for that reason -- a tree-wide default would hide
# which package actually has the old declaration.
- stage: Build
  name: configure
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cmake -S /build/src/libqrencode -B /build/b/libqrencode -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -DCMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR=lib -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/build/sysroot/usr -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH=/build/sysroot -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=None -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=@CC@ -DCMAKE_AR=@AR@ -DCMAKE_RANLIB=@RANLIB@ -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=@CFLAGS_RSP_LIBQRENCODE@ -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS=@LDFLAGS_RSP_LIBQRENCODE@ -DCMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS=@LDFLAGS_RSP_LIBQRENCODE@ -DCMAKE_POLICY_DEFAULT_CMP0056=NEW -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON -DWITH_TOOLS=NO -DWITH_TESTS=NO

- stage: Build
  name: compile
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cmake --build /build/b/libqrencode -j @JOBS@

# The sysroot install goes through make rather than `cmake --install --prefix`.
# DESTDIR=/dest is in the environment of every step, and cmake prepends it to
# whatever --prefix says: that spelling wrote the whole tree into
# /dest/build/sysroot/usr and left the sysroot empty, so a sibling in the same
# bundle linked against nothing. A variable set on make's command line is
# exported, which is the DESTDIR cmake_install.cmake actually reads.
- stage: Build
  name: install
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cmake --install /build/b/libqrencode
  - make -C /build/b/libqrencode install DESTDIR=/build/sysroot
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/stage-sysroot.sh /build/sysroot

  # libqrencode is on the same drops: [visibility] exception and gets the
  # same two-part assertion: the API symbol says visibility was fixed,
  # __cfi_check says the schemes are still on.
- stage: Test
  name: staged
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  # These two are not a sample, they are the whole set: systemd dlopens
  # "libqrencode.so.4" (falling back to .so.3) and asks dlsym for
  # QRcode_encodeString and QRcode_free and nothing else
  # (src/shared/qrcode-util.c:40, at the pinned v261 tag). Because it dlopens
  # rather than links, an empty libqrencode fails no link here -- the recovery
  # key would simply stop being displayed as a QR code on a running system.
  # The .so.4 test is the name dlopen is given; a soname bump upstream would
  # take the fallback to .so.3 with it and neither would be found. It is 4
  # here because CMakeLists.txt:115 sets SOVERSION ${PROJECT_VERSION_MAJOR}
  # against project(QRencode VERSION 4.1.1) on line 3 -- and that is the
  # pinned bytes rather than a neighbouring release, because sources.lock
  # pins this source as a tag archive, so the tag and the tarball are one tree.
  - test -e /dest/usr/lib/libqrencode.so
  - test -e /dest/usr/lib/libqrencode.so.4
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/check-exports.sh @NM@ /dest/usr/lib/libqrencode.so QRcode_encodeString QRcode_free __cfi_check
